    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e6ae3b9f-0603-5083-81ea-76b03bbe4e44",
      ":question/problem": "In medieval times, there were $17380$ people living in a city struck by a plague. If the disease resulted in a daily *decrease* in the population at a continuously compounded rate of $12.3\\%$, what was the city's population after $1$ weeks? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The continuously compounded growth equation is $P=P_0e^{rt}$. The initial amount is $P_0=17380$. Since the amount decreases, the signed decimal rate is $r=-12.3/100=-0.123$. The rate is daily, so convert the duration to days: $t=1\\times 7=7$ days.\n\nSubstitute these values and round only the final amount:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP &= P_0e^{rt} \\\\\n&= 17380e^{(-0.123)(7)} \\\\\n&= 17380e^{-0.861} \\\\\n&= 17380(0.4227391\\ldots) \\\\\n&\\approx 7347\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter $1$ weeks, the population was approximately $7347$ people.",
